
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="description" content="Stage 13: retrospective temporal evaluation of candidate fraud detection models, threshold trade-offs, classification errors, and feature importance.">
  <title>Stage 13 | Fraud Model Retraining Experiment</title>

  <style>
    :root {
      color-scheme: light;
      --page: #f3f7fd;
      --surface: #ffffff;
      --ink: #18263d;
      --muted: #52627a;
      --primary: #174ea6;
      --border: #d7e2f0;
      --info-bg: #edf5ff;
      --warning-bg: #fff7e6;
      --warning-border: #e6b450;
      --radius: 14px;
    }

    * { box-sizing: border-box; }

    body {
      margin: 0;
      padding: 32px 20px 48px;
      background: var(--page);
      color: var(--ink);
      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
      font-size: 16px;
      line-height: 1.65;
    }

    main {
      max-width: 1100px;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }

    header {
      padding: 30px;
      margin-bottom: 22px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ffffff, #eaf3ff);
    }

    h1, h2 { line-height: 1.3; }

    h1 {
      margin: 0 0 12px;
      color: #173b70;
      font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    }

    h2 {
      margin-top: 0;
      color: #173b70;
      font-size: 1.25rem;
    }

    p { margin: 10px 0; }

    .subtitle, .muted { color: var(--muted); }

    .status {
      display: inline-block;
      margin-top: 12px;
      padding: 5px 11px;
      border: 1px solid #b8d2f5;
      border-radius: 999px;
      background: var(--info-bg);
      color: #174ea6;
      font-size: 0.88rem;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 18px;
    }

    section {
      min-width: 0;
      padding: 24px;
      margin: 18px 0;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      background: var(--surface);
      box-shadow: 0 3px 12px rgb(24 38 61 / 4%);
    }

    .grid section { margin: 0; }

    .links {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 10px 16px;
      margin-top: 18px;
    }

    a {
      color: var(--primary);
      font-weight: 700;
      text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }

    a:hover { color: #0b3578; }

    a:focus-visible {
      outline: 3px solid #80b5ff;
      outline-offset: 4px;
      border-radius: 3px;
    }

    .notice {
      border-left: 5px solid var(--warning-border);
      background: var(--warning-bg);
    }

    .download-list {
      padding-left: 22px;
    }

    .download-list li { margin: 9px 0; }

    footer {
      padding: 14px 4px;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.9rem;
    }

    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      body { padding: 18px 12px 32px; }
      header, section { padding: 20px; }
      .grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<main>
  <header>
    <h1>Stage 13 — Fraud Model Retraining Experiment</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      Retrospective evaluation of candidate fraud-detection models using
      chronological training, validation, and final-test partitions.
    </p>
    <span class="status">Research experiment — not a production deployment</span>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Experiment overview</h2>
    <p><strong>Source file:</strong> <code>data\raw\creditcard.csv</code></p>
    <p><strong>Features:</strong> 29</p>
    <p><strong>Split design:</strong> chronological 70% / 15% / 15%</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Observed final-test average precision:</strong>
      Random Forest ranked highest among the candidates in this experiment.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      This is a descriptive result for the reported dataset and split. It does
      not establish that the model will perform similarly on future transactions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Stage 13 analysis dashboard</h2>
    <p>
      Open each interactive chart to explore the results, or use its dedicated
      analysis page for methodology, interpretation, findings, and limitations.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    <section>
      <h2>1. Final Test — Average Precision</h2>
      <p>
        Compare candidate models using average precision, a useful summary
        measure for precision–recall performance on an imbalanced dataset.
      </p>
      <div class="links">
        <a href="analysis_pages/01_final_test_pr_auc_analysis.html">View Full Analysis</a>
        <a href="final_test_pr_auc.html">Open Interactive Chart</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>2. Precision–Recall Operating Trade-off</h2>
      <p>
        Explore how changing the alert threshold affects precision and recall.
        Threshold choice should reflect the operational costs of missed fraud
        and false alerts.
      </p>
      <div class="links">
        <a href="analysis_pages/02_final_test_precision_recall_analysis.html">View Full Analysis</a>
        <a href="final_test_precision_recall.html">Open Interactive Chart</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>3. False Positives and False Negatives</h2>
      <p>
        Examine the trade-off between fraud cases missed by the model and
        legitimate transactions incorrectly flagged for review.
      </p>
      <div class="links">
        <a href="analysis_pages/03_final_test_errors_analysis.html">View Full Analysis</a>
        <a href="final_test_errors.html">Open Interactive Chart</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>4. Model-Specific Feature Importance</h2>
      <p>
        Inspect the relative importance assigned to numeric inputs by the
        fitted candidate models. Feature importance is not, by itself, proof
        of causality or a complete explanation of an individual prediction.
      </p>
      <div class="links">
        <a href="analysis_pages/04_feature_importance_analysis.html">View Full Analysis</a>
        <a href="feature_importance.html">Open Interactive Chart</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>Download aggregate research outputs</h2>
    <p>
      These links provide summary results and experiment documentation.
      Record-level final-test predictions are intentionally not linked here.
    </p>
    <ul class="download-list">
      <li>
        <a href="../../reports/stage13/stage13_results.xlsx">
          Excel Results Workbook
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="../../reports/stage13/stage13_manifest.json">
          Run Manifest
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="../../reports/stage13/tables/stage13_split_summary.csv">
          Temporal Split Summary
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="../../reports/stage13/tables/stage13_model_comparison.csv">
          Aggregate Model Metrics
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="../../reports/stage13/tables/stage13_threshold_selection.csv">
          Validation Threshold Sweep
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="notice">
    <h2>Important methodological limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Retrospective comparison:</strong> the final-test results have
        been examined for this report. Do not continue to describe this test
        set as untouched or repeatedly use it to tune and select models.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Temporal validity:</strong> verify the source timestamps,
        timestamp ties at split boundaries, and whether features would have
        been available at the actual prediction time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Data provenance:</strong> verify dataset origin, licence,
        permitted redistribution, and attribution requirements before release.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Operational validation:</strong> confirm performance on
        genuinely future data, monitor calibration and alert volume, and assess
        review capacity before considering deployment.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Interpretation:</strong> performance metrics and feature
        importance do not alone establish fairness, causal relationships,
        explainability, or production readiness.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>No model is automatically deployed by this experiment.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <footer>
    Stage 13 — Fraud Model Retraining Experiment.
    Interpret results in the context of the documented data, split design,
    model assumptions, and limitations.
  </footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
